+## Progress: 2026-09-18 tick 4 — step 4 done (CPU-only, no Kaggle GPU used)
+
+Decided the int8 quant scheme (step 1 of the next-steps list above, was
+still open): **per-output-channel (per weight-row) symmetric int8**,
+`scale = max(abs(row)) / 127`, round-half-away-from-zero, clamp to
+`[-127, 127]` (Q8_0-style, -128 deliberately unused so the range is
+exactly symmetric). Weight-only quantization (activations stay fp32),
+dequant done **on-the-fly** inside the matmul's inner loop, per the
+design doc's own preference (on-load-to-fp32 would only test I/O, not
+arithmetic).
+
+Implemented in `gpu/cis2_math.h`:
+- `cis2_quantize_row_i8(row, k, q_out) -> scale`: computes max-abs over
+  the row, derives the scale, quantizes each element with the existing
+  `cis2_round_f64` round-half-away-from-zero helper (widened to f64 and
+  back, matching this file's own precedent rather than inventing a new
+  rounding routine), clamped to `[-127, 127]`.
+- `cis2_matmul_batched_int8(B, n, k, x, w_i8, w_scale, out)`: for each
+  (row, output-column) pair, dequantizes each weight element on the fly
+  (`(float)w_i8[i] * scale`, one separately-rounded fp32 multiply, no
+  FMA per this file's house rule) and accumulates with the SAME strict
+  left-to-right sequential order as `cis2_dot_seq` (separate mul, then
+  separate add) — so any digest difference vs the fp32 reference is
+  attributable only to quantization, not to a second independent
+  reduction-order change (isolates the variable under test, per the
+  design doc's own step-2/step-3 methodology).
+
+Extended `gpu/test_batch.cpp` (same file, no new source) so that when
+built with `-DCIS2_BATCH_INT8` it additionally: quantizes the SAME
+deterministic weight matrix `w` used by steps 2/3 (row by row), runs
+`cis2_matmul_batched_int8` against the SAME activations `x`, and
+compares its digest against `out_seq` (the fp32 batch=1-equivalent
+reference), reporting digest, bitwise-diff count, and max/mean absolute
+difference — without changing step 2's own pass/fail exit code (a step-4
+mismatch is a documented finding, not a build failure, mirroring step
+3's precedent). Added the `test_batch_int8` Makefile target (same
+pattern as `test_batch_grouped`).
+
+**Result: MISMATCH, as expected a priori (quantization error, not a
+bug).** Command run: `make -f gpu/Makefile test_batch_int8` (from repo
+root). Real output:
+
+```
+BATCH_TEST B=4 n=37 k=129 batched_digest=d241d2052ee1f4a6236426509d35cb069c17f477405ffa6cb31d1db9ac3493ba seq_digest=d241d2052ee1f4a6236426509d35cb069c17f477405ffa6cb31d1db9ac3493ba bitwise_diffs=0/148
+BATCH_TEST RESULT: MATCH
+BATCH_INT8_TEST B=4 n=37 k=129 int8_digest=8ef80ce87fa727b1e3de8b3d92a944d7d55ee739a3101d5fbbc157e786b03c41 seq_digest=d241d2052ee1f4a6236426509d35cb069c17f477405ffa6cb31d1db9ac3493ba bitwise_diffs_vs_seq=148/148 max_abs_diff=0.16224861 mean_abs_diff=0.04701660
+BATCH_INT8_TEST RESULT vs seq: MISMATCH
+```
+
+So: step 2's own row-batch check still passes (MATCH, unchanged, confirms
+step 4's additions did not disturb it — verified by re-running both
+`test_batch` and `test_batch_grouped` after this change, both still
+report their prior digests/results unchanged, same
+`d241d205...`/`d0e4116d...` pair as tick 3), and the int8 digest
+(`8ef80ce8...`) differs from the fp32 sequential reference (`d241d205...`)
